{
  "title": "Iterative refinement: solve cheaply, correct accurately",
  "narration": "Now for the key trick in the paper, iterative refinement. We want to solve A u equals b, but our factorization is only half precision, so each solve is a little off. Here A is the matrix four, one, one, three, and b is five, four. The exact answer is one and one. [[start]]We begin with the guess u equal to zero. [[resid]]Next we compute the residual, r equals b minus A times u, and we do this part in higher precision. With u equal to zero, the residual is just five and four. [[solve1]]Now we use the cheap half precision factorization to solve for a correction. It is not exact, so it returns zero point nine, zero point nine, instead of one, one. [[upd1]]We add it to u, and recompute the residual: zero point five and zero point four. It shrank by a factor of ten. [[solve2]]Repeat. The cheap solve now gives a correction of zero point zero nine, and [[upd2]]u becomes zero point nine nine, zero point nine nine, with residual zero point zero five and zero point zero four. Each pass cuts the residual by the same factor, as long as the factorization is a decent approximation. We stop when the residual is small enough.",
